Similarly to the previous case, we can assume that $v_a, v_b \in \{0, 1\}$. 
oindent	extbf{Case 1} Suppose $v_a=v_b=0$. Then $a=u_a$ and $b=u_b$ are units. The claim is $(u_a,u_b)=(-1)^{\epsilon(u_a)\epsilon(u_b)}$, which is $1$ exactly when at least one of $u_a$ and $u_b$ is congruent to $1$ modulo $4$ and $-1$ otherwise. Suppose first, without loss of generality, that $u_a \equiv 1 \pmod 4$. Then its residue class modulo $8$ is either $1$ or $5$. In the first case, $u_a$ is a square, so $(u_a,u_b)=1$. In the second case, $u_a+4u_b \equiv 1 \pmod 8$, so it is the square of some $w in \mathbb{Z}_2$, so \[ w^2-u_a\cdot 1^2- u_b\cdot 2^2 = 0 \] and thus $(u_a,u_b)=1$. If $u_a \equiv u_b \equiv -1 \pmod 4$, then suppose there exist $(x,y,z)$ not all zero, coprime, such that \[ z^2-u_ax^2-u_by^2=0. \] Reducing modulo $4$, we obtain $z^2+x^2+y^2 \equiv 0 \pmod 4$, which is impossible, since the only squares modulo $4$ are $0$ and $1$, and the sum can be $0$ only if $x,y,z$ are all even, contradicting the fact that they are coprime. Thus, $(u_a,u_b)=-1$. This proof uses \lstinline{ZMod.exists_sq_eq_two_iff} from \lstinline{Mathlib.NumberTheory.LegendreSymbol.QuadraticReciprocity} and \lstinline{hensels_lemma} in \lstinline{Mathlib.NumberTheory.Padics.Hensel} 
oindent	extbf{Case 2} Suppose $v_a=1$ and $v_b=0$. Then $a=2 u_a$ and $b=u_b$. We first show that \[ (2, u_b) = (-1)^{\omega(u_b)}, \] i.e. that $(2, u_b)=1$ if an only if $u_b \equiv \pm 1 \pmod 8$. Suppose that $(2, u_b)=1$. Equivalently, there exist $x,y,z$ not all zero, coprime, such that \[ z^2-2x^2-u_by^2=0. \] By Lemma ef{lem:nontrivialzero}, we can assume that $z$ and $y$ are units. Reducing modulo $8$, we get $1 - 2x^2 - u_b \equiv 0 \pmod 8$. Since squares modulo $8$ are $0, 1$ and $4$, we have $2x^2 \equiv 0$ or $2 \pmod 8$, so $u_b \equiv 1$ or $-1 \pmod 8$. Conversely, if $u_b \equiv 1 \pmod 8$, then it is a square, so $(2, u_b)=1$. If $u_b \equiv -1 \pmod 8$, then $z^2-2x^2-u_by^2=0$ has a solution modulo $8$, namely $(1,1,1)$, which can be lifted to a solution in $\mathbb{Z}_2$ by Hensel's lemma (ef{lem:multivariable_hensel}), so $(2, u_b)=1$. Now, if we prove that $(2u_a,u_b)=(u_a,u_b)(2,u_b)$, then we are done. If either factor is $1$, by Proposition ef{prop:hilbert_symbol_properties}, we are done. Otherwise, both factors are $-1$, so by the above we know $u_a \equiv -1 \pmod 4$ and $u_b \equiv 3 \pmod 8$. Up to multiplying by squares, we can consider the following two cases:  egin{itemize} \item $u_a=-1,u_b=3$. Then $(1,1,1)$ is a nontrivial solution to $z^2+2x^2-3y^2=0$. \item $u_a=3,u_b=-5$. Then $(1,1,1)$ is a nontrivial solution to $z^2-6x^2+5y^2=0$. \end{itemize} 
oindent	extbf{Case 3} Suppose $v_a=v_b=1$. Using Proposition ef{prop:hilbert_symbol_properties} and the proof of the previous case, we have \[ (2u_a,2u_b)=(2u_a,-4u_au_b)=(2u_a,-u_au_b)=(-1)^{\epsilon(u_a)\epsilon(-u_au_b)+\omega(-u_au_b)}, \] and a direct calculation shows that \[ \epsilon(u_a)\epsilon(-u_au_b)+\omega(-u_au_b) = \epsilon(u_a)\epsilon(u_b) + \omega(u_b) + \omega(u_a). \]
